WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.009
You will hear some very strange things from
physicists sometimes that according to the

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:10.410
theory of relativity, the passage of time
is an illusion, that time doesn't really change,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:18.320
that there really is no motion, that everything
is somehow existing all together and frozen.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.720
I find those claims to be, frankly, incredible
and would be more amazing a discovery than

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.610
anything that's happened in the history of
mankind to find out that all of change and

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:33.780
all of time and the passage of time doesn't
really happen.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.030
I've been asking myself: How did people come
to think that?

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:43.940
And I think the answer is this: That when
we think about space and time in general relativity,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.350
we try to use the geometrical tools and the
mathematical tools that were developed for

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:56.180
expressing geometrical structure, and that
those tools were originally developed just

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:01.260
for space, like for Euclidean geometry for
understanding the structure of space

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.280
around us.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.660
And in space there is no direction.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:13.180
There is no fundamental, intrinsic directionality
in spatial structure, so you wouldn't naturally

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:17.490
develop mathematical tools to represent intrinsic
directionality.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:23.619
And what happened is taking those tools that
were developed for one purpose, you then use

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:25.240
them for this other purpose.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.970
For not only describing space but time, and
you end up saying things like, "Oh time is

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.930
just another dimension like space."

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.759
Time is spatialized.

00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:38.049
In doing so, you loose track of the directionality
of time.

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:43.990
You loose track of the fact that time is fundamentally
different from space in that it has an intrinsic

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:44.990
direction to it.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:49.530
It goes from the past to the future, and we
would never say space goes from the East to

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.719
the West or the top to the bottom any more
than the bottom to the top.

00:01:54.719 --> 00:02:01.350
What I've been doing recently is developing
a different mathematical way of representing

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:06.170
geometrical structure that has directionality
built into it in a very natural way, that

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:13.220
you can represent both things that have an
intrinsic directionality and things that don't.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:18.671
And in that picture it looks like the right
thing to say about relativity is not that

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:24.920
is spatializes time which makes the direction
of time very puzzling, but that it temporalizes

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.600
space, that really what's fundamental is temporal
structure, is time structure, and that in

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.000
a certain sense spatial structure derives
from that.

